# The release this tree belongs to.
#
# mmo_server and mmo_client are two independent git repositories with no
# submodule, no lockfile and no tag linking them. They share a wire format
# (protocol.h), a world file format (world_format.h) and a set of content
# checks -- and until this file existed, nothing recorded which server build a
# given client build was ever meant to talk to.
#
# What went wrong without it: check_protocol_sync.sh only compares the two
# copies of protocol.h when they happen to sit side by side in one directory.
# Check out the two trees at unrelated commits and it prints "SKIPPED" and the
# build passes. The mismatch then shows up as a client refused at the handshake
# if PROTOCOL_VERSION happened to differ, or -- far worse -- as a packet
# misread in silence if it did not.
#
# Both trees carry this file. The server tree's tests/check_tree_pairing.sh
# compares them and fails when they disagree; the server's `make test` runs it,
# and so does its CI. Keep this file byte-identical to the server's copy.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing this
#
# Bump `release` in BOTH trees, in the same change, whenever a change to any
# shared surface means an old counterpart can no longer be trusted against a
# new one:
#
#   * protocol.h in either tree (which also bumps PROTOCOL_VERSION)
#   * common/world_format.h / the world.dat layout
#   * anything the cross-tree check scripts compare
#
# The value is opaque. It is compared for equality and nothing else -- it is
# not ordered, not parsed, and carries no meaning beyond "these two trees were
# released together". A date plus a serial reads well in a log line and sorts
# usefully for a human, which is the only reason for the format.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

release = 2026.08.22-1
